{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1001375-the-iron-hand-of-gotz-von-berlichingen","id":1001375,"title":"The Iron Hand of Gotz von Berlichingen","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-11-10T09:19:54-08:00","id":1001375,"title":"The Iron Hand of Gotz von Berlichingen","content":"Did you know that there was a real life man with a clockwork metal prosthetic hand...<b><i>made in 1504</i></b>?<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/G%C3%B6tz_von_Berlichingen\" target=\"_blank\">Gotz von Berlichingen</a> - German Imperial Knight, mercenary, and poet - would already be an interesting historical figure in his own right. He'd fit right in with any Sorcerer's Crusade character, even if he didn't have magick. But he was also famous for losing his hand to a cannonball, and promptly getting an <a href=\"http://coilhouse.net/2008/03/the-iron-hand-of-gotz-von-berlichingen/\" target=\"_blank\">iron mechanical prosthetic</a>.<br /> <br /> Again, <i>in 1504</i>.<br /> <br /> So the obvious question is...who among the Artificers made it (or rather them, since he had at least two) for him? And is the fact that modern culture has forgotten him a result of the NWO's tampering with the historical record? Actually, that second question isn't a question. The real question is why the Order of Reason/Technocracy would suppress knowledge of this fact, thereby delaying the Union's ability to spread articulated mechanical prosthetics to the masses? When I say suppressed, I'm obviously being relative, seeing that Goethe wrote a play about Gotz of the Iron Hand. I mean suppressed in the sense that something this amazing is so downplayed.<br /> <br /> Thoughts?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-10T12:01:02-08:00","id":1001417,"title":"","content":"He's a better known figure in certain groups than others? I've known about him for ages. As well, he's a partial inspiration for the design of Guts from Berserk (a warrior with a highly functional mechanical hand in a fantasy Medieval style setting), so it seems an odd assertion that he's been forgotten.<br /> <br /> It's also worth remembering that the design wasn't actually that advanced for the times; he could just afford it because it was super expensive. That's the biggest reason such prosthetic designs didn't catch on. Maintain them was beyond most people economically, and didn't necessarily provide significantly greater functionality than many simpler designs. However cool his hand was, you have to remember that each joint had it's own spring-ratchet assembly that had to be set and released individually (though the fingers were a bit easier since you could bend the joints at once). All of which gives little that you can't get out of three hooks and a single tension screw.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}